For $r>0$: $2.0\,\mathrm{A}<I'<3.0\,\mathrm{A}$. Only $2.4\,\mathrm{A}$ fits $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Ammeters and voltmeters}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Ammeter: measures current; connected in \textbf{series}. Ideal ammeter has
        \textbf{very small} resistance (ideally zero).
  \item Voltmeter: measures p.d.; connected in \textbf{parallel}. Ideal voltmeter has
        \textbf{very large} resistance (ideally infinite).
  \item A real ammeter increases total circuit resistance $\Rightarrow$ measured $I$ is slightly smaller.
  \item A real voltmeter loads the circuit $\Rightarrow$ measured $V$ may differ from the unloaded value.
\end{enumerate}

\remark{Exam default: treat meters as ideal unless the question states otherwise.}

%======================================================================
\section{Power and energy}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Power (rate of energy transfer):
        \[
        P=\frac{E}{t}=VI=I^{2}R=\frac{V^{2}}{R}.
        \]
  \item Energy transferred in time $t$: $E=Pt=VIt$.
  \item In a resistor, electrical energy is converted mainly to heat.
  \item For series / parallel: use the correct $V$ or $I$ for each component when computing $P$.
\end{enumerate}

\ex
A $12\,\mathrm{V}$ battery of negligible internal resistance supplies $2.0\,\mathrm{A}$ to a heater for
$5.0$~minutes. Find (a)~the power and (b)~the energy transferred.
\sol
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $P=VI=(12)(2.0)=24\,\mathrm{W}$.
  \item $E=Pt=24\times(5.0\times 60)=7200\,\mathrm{J}$.
\end{enumerate}

